{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2182-05-09 21:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, I'm Dr. Lee, one of the neurology team. I understand you came in because of facial drooping?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2182-05-09 21:32:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, that’s right. I noticed it last night when I was brushing my teeth. The left side of my mouth just felt... loose."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2182-05-09 21:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. Did you have any other symptoms—like weakness, trouble speaking, or vision changes?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2182-05-09 21:35:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "No, not really. I could talk fine, no numbness or weakness in my arms or legs. But this morning, it was worse. And my voice sounded kind of nasally."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2182-05-09 21:37:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Okay. I also see you’ve had a cold and sinus symptoms for about two weeks. Were you on any medication for that?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2182-05-09 21:38:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, I was on doxycycline for 10 days, but I stopped a week ago. My cough and sinus pressure are better, but not completely gone."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2182-05-09 21:40:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Got it. Given the sudden onset of facial droop, we need to rule out a stroke. We’ve ordered a CT angiogram of your head and neck to check for any blockages or bleeding."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2182-05-09 21:42:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "A stroke? But I didn’t have any weakness or confusion. Are you sure?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2182-05-09 21:44:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’s important to be cautious. Facial droop can be a sign of a transient ischemic attack—sometimes called a mini-stroke—even without other symptoms. The scan will help us know for sure."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2182-05-09 23:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your CT scan is back. Good news—there’s no sign of a stroke, bleed, or major blockage in the brain."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2182-05-09 23:17:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Oh, that’s a relief. So what’s causing the droop then?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2182-05-09 23:20:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "The scan did show signs of sinus inflammation—mucosal thickening in your maxillary and ethmoid sinuses. That, combined with your recent infection, makes us think this could be Bell’s palsy, which is facial nerve inflammation often triggered by a viral illness."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2182-05-09 23:22:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Bell’s palsy? I’ve heard of that. Is it serious?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2182-05-09 23:24:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’s usually not dangerous. Most people recover fully over weeks to months. We’ll start you on a steroid to reduce the inflammation and help speed recovery."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2182-05-10 05:40:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We just got your MRI results. It confirms no stroke or brain lesion. The sinus inflammation is still there, but nothing else abnormal."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2182-05-10 05:42:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So it’s definitely Bell’s palsy?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2182-05-10 05:45:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Based on your symptoms, normal imaging, and recent illness, yes, that’s the most likely diagnosis. We’ll keep monitoring you, but you’re responding as expected."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2182-05-10 14:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ve made good progress. Since your symptoms started recently and there’s no stroke, we’re comfortable discharging you today with oral steroids and instructions to follow up with neurology."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2182-05-10 14:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay. Should I be worried about my liver or transplant history?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2182-05-10 14:05:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your transplant team was consulted. They agree your current issue isn’t related to your liver. Just continue your usual medications, including the antiplatelet for your heart health."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2182-05-10 14:07:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And the breast finding on the scan? Should I be concerned?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2182-05-10 14:09:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "There was a small calcified structure noted. It’s not urgent, but we recommend you follow up with your primary doctor for a mammogram to check it out."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2182-05-10 14:11:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Alright, I’ll do that. And if the face doesn’t get better?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2182-05-10 14:13:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Most people improve within three weeks. If not, or if you develop new symptoms, contact neurology right away."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2182-05-10 14:15:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you, doctor. I feel much better knowing it’s not a stroke."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2182-05-10 14:17:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. We’ll get your discharge papers ready. Rest up, take your meds, and follow up as planned."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2182-05-10 14:20:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will do. Thanks again."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2182-05-10 14:22:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Take care. We’ll see you in follow-up if needed."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2182-05-09 21:27:00",
    "admission_end": "2182-05-10 16:17:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A woman with a history of hypertension, primary sclerosing cholangitis, and liver transplant presented with acute left facial droop that began the prior night and worsened by morning. She had no other neurological deficits but reported recent cold and sinus symptoms, for which she had taken doxycycline. Imaging with CTA head and neck showed no evidence of stroke, hemorrhage, or significant vascular stenosis, and a subsequent MRI confirmed no infarct or mass. Findings of acute sinusitis were noted on both scans. The clinical picture was consistent with Bell’s palsy, likely triggered by recent infection. She was treated with steroids, showed stable improvement, and was discharged with neurology follow-up. A small calcified breast lesion was incidentally noted, with recommendation for outpatient breast imaging. Her transplant status was reviewed and deemed unrelated to the current episode.",
    "problems": [
      "Transient cerebral ischemic attack, unspecified",
      "Facial weakness",
      "Other abnormal involuntary movements",
      "Primary sclerosing cholangitis",
      "Liver transplant status",
      "Headache",
      "Tinnitus, unspecified ear",
      "Essential (primary) hypertension",
      "Gastro-esophageal reflux disease without esophagitis",
      "Hypothyroidism, unspecified",
      "Celiac disease",
      "Personal history of nicotine dependence",
      "Type 2 diabetes mellitus without complications",
      "Other specified disorders of breast",
      "Long term (current) use of antithrombotics/antiplatelets"
    ]
  }
}
